    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1089">

                <pb facs="0992" n="984" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg984"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.417/1082: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-03-19T17:00:00+09:00">March 19, 1940—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 19—6:40 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>184. The press reports that the Minister for Foreign Affairs<anchor n="43"
                        xml:id="d1089-f1"/> stated on March 18, in a committee of the Lower House
                    (when complaint was made by a member that the protection afforded by the Fur
                    Seals Convention of 1911<anchor n="44" xml:id="d1089-f2"/> had so increased the
                    number of seals that the fisheries were being seriously affected), the [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">that?</hi>] consideration is now being given to
                    proposing in the near future a revision of the Convention. He is reported to
                    have added, “The Convention may now be terminated but I would like first to give
                    the matter further study.”</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1089-f1" n="43" xml:id="d1089-f1-content">
                <p>Hachiro Arita.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1089-f2" n="44" xml:id="d1089-f2-content">
                <p>Signed at Washington, July 7, 1911, by the United States, Great Britain, Japan,
                    and Russia, <ref target="frus1911#pg_260"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign
                            Relations</hi>, 1911, p. 260</ref>.</p>
